doc_id: repository-patterns
title: Repository Layer Patterns
project: ai-workflow
version_hint: "2024-2025"
kind: data_access_layer_standards
description: Repository (data-access) layer patterns providing storage-agnostic abstraction over SurrealDB, Elasticsearch, and future data stores, consumed by services rather than routers.
scope: Covers repository responsibilities, interface design, implementation classes, SurrealDB patterns, Elasticsearch patterns, transaction management, query patterns, error handling, testing strategies, factory/DI, generic repositories, and anti-patterns. Service orchestration and router concerns are in separate documents.
primary_runtime_references[4]:
  - path: app/repositories/example_repository.py
    role: Repository implementation example.
  - path: app/infrastructure/db_connections.py
    role: Connection pool management.
  - path: app/infrastructure/surrealdb/pool.py
    role: SurrealDB pool implementation.
  - path: app/infrastructure/elasticsearch/client.py
    role: Elasticsearch wrapper.
transitional_note: References to .md files (connection-pooling-patterns.md, dependency-patterns.md) are transitional and will be updated to .toon filenames once those documents are converted in later phases.
related_pattern_docs[5]:
  - service-patterns.toon
  - router-patterns.toon
  - connection-pooling-patterns.md
  - dependency-patterns.md
  - api-patterns.toon
sections[12]:
  - id: responsibilities
    index: 1
    title: Repository responsibilities
    category: layer_definition
    summary: Repositories encapsulate all direct interaction with databases and search indices for a given domain aggregate or entity.
    items[7]:
      - id: responsibilities.persistence-abstraction
        type: rule
        text: Hide SurrealDB SQL, Elasticsearch queries, or future storage details behind a stable Python interface.
      - id: responsibilities.service-facing
        type: rule
        text: Services depend on repository interfaces, not on infrastructure classes like SurrealDBPool or ElasticsearchWrapper.
      - id: responsibilities.domain-mapping
        type: rule
        text: Return domain models or DTOs (Pydantic models in app/contracts/), not raw database rows or driver-specific response structures.
      - id: responsibilities.testability
        type: rule
        text: Make it straightforward to substitute real implementations with fakes or mocks for fast, isolated testing of business logic.
      - id: responsibilities.crud-operations
        type: rule
        text: Implement CRUD and query operations (get_by_id, list, create, update, delete) along with aggregate-specific queries.
      - id: responsibilities.http-agnostic
        type: rule
        text: Repositories are unaware of HTTP; they never import FastAPI or raise HTTP-specific exceptions.
      - id: responsibilities.architecture
        type: note
        text: Modern FastAPI architectures pair repositories with a dedicated service layer; services compose multiple repositories and own business rules.
  - id: interface-design
    index: 2
    title: Repository interface design
    category: standard
    summary: Define repository interfaces using typing.Protocol for clear contracts without forcing inheritance.
    sample_code:
      description: Example repository protocol defining async CRUD operations.
      imports[2]: typing.Protocol,app.contracts.users.User
      components[1]:
        - type: protocol
          name: UserRepositoryProtocol
          methods[6]:
            - signature: "async def get_by_id(self, user_id: str) -> User | None"
              docstring: Return a user by ID, or None if not found.
            - signature: "async def get_by_field(self, field_name: str, value: str) -> list[User]"
              docstring: Return users matching a simple equality filter on a field.
            - signature: "async def list(self, offset: int = 0, limit: int = 100) -> list[User]"
              docstring: Return a window of users for pagination.
            - signature: "async def create(self, user: User) -> User"
              docstring: Persist a new user and return the stored record.
            - signature: "async def update(self, user: User) -> User"
              docstring: Persist an updated user and return the stored record.
            - signature: "async def delete(self, user_id: str) -> None"
              docstring: Delete a user by ID; succeed silently if already absent.
    items[6]:
      - id: interface.per-entity
        type: rule
        text: Prefer one repository interface per aggregate (e.g., UserRepositoryProtocol, TicketRepositoryProtocol) instead of a single catch-all.
      - id: interface.typed-crud
        type: rule
        text: All methods must have explicit type hints for parameters and return values.
      - id: interface.async
        type: rule
        text: Use async def for all I/O-bound operations so repositories compose naturally with async services and FastAPI routes.
      - id: interface.protocol
        type: rule
        text: Protocols describe the contract; concrete classes implement them in app/repositories/ or domain-specific modules.
      - id: interface.standard-surface
        type: note
        text: "A typical repository interface exposes get_by_id, get_by_field, list, create, update, delete, plus domain-specific query methods. See app/repositories/example_repository.py for reference."
      - id: interface.current-reference
        type: note
        text: "The repository pattern is illustrated in app/repositories/example_repository.py, where ExampleRepositoryProtocol is implemented against SurrealDBPool for save_processed_message. Services such as ExampleService in app/services/example_service.py depend on the protocol instead of the concrete pool, and API v1 wiring in app/api/v1/dependencies.py provides the repository via get_example_repository for service construction."
  - id: implementation-classes
    index: 3
    title: Implementing repository classes
    category: standard
    summary: Implement one or more concrete repository classes per protocol, encapsulating specific storage technology.
    sample_code:
      description: Repository constructor pattern with pool injection.
      imports[1]: app.infrastructure.db_connections.SurrealDBPool
      components[1]:
        - type: class
          name: SurrealUserRepository
          implements: UserRepositoryProtocol
          constructor: "def __init__(self, pool: SurrealDBPool) -> None:\n    self._pool = pool"
    items[4]:
      - id: impl.one-per-aggregate
        type: rule
        text: Create separate repositories for distinct aggregates rather than a monolithic repository handling every table or index.
      - id: impl.constructor-injection
        type: rule
        text: Repository constructors accept minimal infrastructure dependencies (SurrealDBPool, ElasticsearchWrapper, AsyncSession); they do not reach into global application state.
      - id: impl.async-access
        type: rule
        text: All methods that hit external stores must be async and rely on async-aware primitives from the underlying client or wrapper.
      - id: impl.no-business-logic
        type: rule
        text: Repositories translate calls into query operations and map results; they do not implement cross-aggregate rules or workflows.
  - id: surrealdb-pattern
    index: 4
    title: SurrealDB repository pattern
    category: standard
    summary: SurrealDB access is coordinated via SurrealDBPool; repositories use parameterized queries and connection acquisition.
    sample_code:
      description: SurrealDB query pattern inside a repository method.
      code: "async def get_by_id(self, user_id: str) -> User | None:\n    sql = \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $id LIMIT 1;\"\n    params = {\"id\": user_id}\n    async with self._pool.acquire() as conn:\n        result = await conn.query(sql, params)\n    # Map SurrealDB result into a User model\n    ..."
    items[5]:
      - id: surrealdb.connection
        type: rule
        text: Use async with pool.acquire() as conn to obtain connections; the pool handles timeouts and returning connections to the queue.
      - id: surrealdb.parameterized
        type: rule
        text: Call await conn.query(sql, params) with parameter dictionaries instead of interpolating values into SQL strings to avoid injection.
      - id: surrealdb.schema
        type: rule
        text: Schema and migration operations are centralized on SurrealDBPool via helpers like initialize_schema() and execute_schema(). Repositories should not define or migrate schema.
      - id: surrealdb.autocommit
        type: note
        text: In the current architecture, SurrealDB queries are auto-committed by the driver. Transaction boundary guidance prepares for future transactional stores.
      - id: surrealdb.mapping
        type: rule
        text: Repositories encapsulate mapping from SurrealDB records to domain models, shielding services from driver-specific result shapes.
  - id: elasticsearch-pattern
    index: 5
    title: Elasticsearch repository pattern
    category: standard
    summary: Search-oriented repositories depend on ElasticsearchWrapper for async-friendly methods and index initialization.
    sample_code:
      description: Elasticsearch search repository pattern.
      imports[1]: app.infrastructure.db_connections.ElasticsearchWrapper
      components[1]:
        - type: class
          name: FactsSearchRepository
          constructor: "def __init__(self, es: ElasticsearchWrapper) -> None:\n    self._es = es"
          methods[1]:
            - name: search_by_text
              signature: "async def search_by_text(self, text: str) -> list[Fact]"
              body: "query = {\"match\": {\"text\": text}}\nraw = await self._es.search(index=\"facts_index\", query=query)\n# Map hits to Fact models\n..."
    items[6]:
      - id: es.wrapper-methods
        type: rule
        text: Use search, index, bulk, and create_index on ElasticsearchWrapper rather than calling the Elasticsearch client directly.
      - id: es.async-threads
        type: note
        text: The wrapper executes blocking client calls via anyio.to_thread.run_sync, allowing repository methods to remain async.
      - id: es.index-init
        type: rule
        text: Indices like facts_index and entity_aliases_index are created during application startup via initialize_indices(). Repositories may assume these indices exist.
      - id: es.search-vs-write
        type: note
        text: Some repositories only perform searches (read-only reporting) while others support indexing and bulk operations.
      - id: es.index-names
        type: rule
        text: Centralize index names and query shapes in repositories so they remain discoverable and consistent.
      - id: es.alternative-example
        type: note
        text: "Similarly, an ElasticsearchUserSearchRepository could depend on an ElasticsearchWrapper to provide search capabilities over user indices."
  - id: transaction-management
    index: 6
    title: Transaction management
    category: standard
    summary: Repositories must not commit or roll back transactions; they participate in transactions defined by services.
    items[4]:
      - id: txn.service-boundaries
        type: rule
        text: Services determine when a logical unit of work begins and ends; they may wrap repository calls in async with blocks or unit-of-work abstractions.
      - id: txn.relational-future
        type: note
        text: If a relational database and ORM (e.g., SQLAlchemy) are introduced, repositories may accept an AsyncSession and call session.flush() while leaving session.commit() to the service.
      - id: txn.current-behavior
        type: note
        text: With SurrealDB and Elasticsearch, each operation is effectively auto-committed. This document treats transaction boundaries as a service-level concern for architectural consistency.
      - id: txn.cross-aggregate
        type: rule
        text: "When a use case spans multiple repositories, the service layer coordinates all calls and defines recovery behavior if one step fails. See service-patterns.toon for orchestration details."
  - id: query-patterns
    index: 7
    title: Query patterns and pagination
    category: standard
    summary: Repositories define the low-level query surface with safe filtering, pagination, and domain model mapping.
    sample_code:
      description: List method signature with pagination parameters.
      code: "async def list(self, offset: int = 0, limit: int = 100) -> list[User]: ..."
    items[5]:
      - id: query.parameterized
        type: rule
        text: Always prefer parameterized queries ($id, $namespace, etc.) with separate parameter dictionaries to avoid injection and make queries reusable.
      - id: query.pagination
        type: rule
        text: "Repository methods should accept offset and limit integers for result windows. These map to API concepts like page and page_size (offset = (page - 1) * page_size)."
      - id: query.domain-results
        type: rule
        text: Methods return lists of domain models, not raw database rows or Elasticsearch hits. Mapping is the repository's responsibility.
      - id: query.filtering
        type: rule
        text: Expose explicit filter and sort parameters rather than accepting raw where clauses or query dicts from callers.
      - id: query.bulk
        type: rule
        text: When the underlying store exposes efficient bulk APIs (e.g., Elasticsearch bulk), provide repository-level methods like bulk_index or bulk_insert.
  - id: error-handling
    index: 8
    title: Error handling in repositories
    category: standard
    summary: Translate expected conditions into domain exceptions; let unexpected infrastructure errors propagate.
    items[4]:
      - id: errors.expected
        type: rule
        text: When a domain entity is not found or a known constraint is violated, raise specific exceptions like ResourceNotFoundError or DuplicateRecordError derived from DomainError in app/core/errors.py.
      - id: errors.unexpected
        type: rule
        text: Do not catch broad exceptions like Exception to hide database connectivity issues, timeouts, or driver bugs; let these propagate for global error handlers.
      - id: errors.logging
        type: rule
        text: When catching and translating expected errors, log only high-level context (entity type, identifier); avoid including sensitive data or raw query text.
      - id: errors.http-mapping
        type: note
        text: Mapping domain exceptions to HTTP status codes and error payloads is the responsibility of exception-handling layer documented in exception-patterns.md and api-patterns.toon.
  - id: testing-strategies
    index: 9
    title: Repository testing strategies
    category: standard
    summary: Test repositories at multiple levels from unit tests with mocks to integration tests with real stores.
    items[4]:
      - id: testing.unit
        type: rule
        text: Replace SurrealDBPool or ElasticsearchWrapper with fakes or mocks that capture executed queries without performing real I/O. Assert on SQL strings, parameter dictionaries, or search bodies.
      - id: testing.integration
        type: rule
        text: Use test databases or indices to verify that repository methods interact correctly with actual storage engines.
      - id: testing.fixtures
        type: note
        text: The fixtures in tests/conftest.py demonstrate application composition for integration and E2E tests. Repository tests can reuse this composition root.
      - id: testing.isolation
        type: rule
        text: "Ensure test data is reset between runs by truncating collections, deleting indices, or using transactional rollbacks. See testing-patterns.md for concrete reset strategies."
  - id: factory-di
    index: 10
    title: Repository factory and dependency injection
    category: standard
    summary: Construct repositories via factory functions compatible with FastAPI dependency injection and the application factory in app/core/factory.py.
    sample_code:
      description: Factory-style dependency pattern for repository injection.
      imports[1]: fastapi.Depends
      code: "def get_user_repository() -> UserRepositoryProtocol:\n    pool = ...  # Retrieve SurrealDBPool from app state or another dependency\n    return SurrealUserRepository(pool)\n\nasync def get_user_service(\n    user_repo: UserRepositoryProtocol = Depends(get_user_repository),\n) -> UserService:\n    return UserService(user_repo=user_repo)"
    items[4]:
      - id: factory.functions
        type: rule
        text: Define simple functions like def get_user_repository(pool) -> UserRepositoryProtocol that build concrete repositories.
      - id: factory.app-state
        type: rule
        text: create_app stores initialized SurrealDBPool and ElasticsearchWrapper on app.state. Dependency functions retrieve these and pass them into repository factories.
      - id: factory.fastapi-deps
        type: rule
        text: Use Depends with repository factory functions to inject repositories into services or route handlers. FastAPI per-request dependency cache ensures each repository is constructed at most once per request.
      - id: factory.service-composition
        type: rule
        text: "Services receive repositories via constructors (see service-patterns.toon), keeping dependencies explicit and testable."
  - id: generic-repositories
    index: 11
    title: Generic repositories
    category: optional
    summary: Generic base repositories can reduce duplication but should be used sparingly to avoid obscuring behavior.
    items[4]:
      - id: generic.shared-crud
        type: note
        text: "If many entities share identical CRUD semantics, consider extracting a base class like GenericRepository[T] that implements get_by_id, list, create, delete."
      - id: generic.overrides
        type: rule
        text: Concrete repositories may override specific methods to optimize queries, enforce additional constraints, or integrate search backends.
      - id: generic.avoid-over-generalization
        type: rule
        text: Overly generic repositories can obscure behavior and make debugging difficult. Favor explicit, per-entity repositories when in doubt.
      - id: generic.guidance
        type: note
        text: "2024 industry best practices recommend explicit repositories per aggregate as the default, with generic abstractions used sparingly."
  - id: anti-patterns
    index: 12
    title: Anti-patterns to avoid
    category: anti_patterns
    summary: Common pitfalls that undermine repository layer design.
    items[6]:
      - id: anti.commit-transactions
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Repositories that commit or roll back transactions instead of delegating transaction control to services.
      - id: anti.business-rules
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Repositories that implement business rules, workflows, or cross-aggregate orchestration.
      - id: anti.raw-responses
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Repositories that return ORM models or raw driver responses (SurrealDB records, Elasticsearch hit structures) instead of domain models or DTOs.
      - id: anti.global-instances
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Global or module-level repository instances that bypass dependency injection and make testing difficult.
      - id: anti.god-repositories
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Single god repositories that handle unrelated aggregates instead of focused, per-entity repositories.
      - id: anti.over-generic
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Over-generic repositories whose type parameters and behavior are difficult to understand at call sites.
